Description: compositor: A mirror of a stereo output copies its eyes instead of painting the scene again
 KWin's mirroring paints the scene once per output, and with stereo eyes once
 per output and eye. Outputs are filters (STEREO3D.md): the scene is composed
 once, as the two eyes, and each output on the way out applies its own layout.
 .
 The output that has the logical output keeps the two eyes of each frame it
 composes while a replica asks for them (StereoEyePair: the shadow buffers
 of the frame, held through their release points). A replica copies each eye
 with a blit into the eye buffers it would have painted the scene into, scaled
 once into the place its view shows the scene, black around it, then runs its
 own layout as always: frame packing, anaglyph, half side by side, rows,
 columns, checkerboard, or the left eye for a 2D screen. The replica makes no
 scene pass; the composing output wakes it when it has new eyes. A replica
 that finds no eyes yet, or cannot use them (another GPU, a rotated output,
 another colour space, an ICC profile, frame sequential, a stereo pair, the
 picture and depth layouts), paints the scene as before, and so does every
 output with KWIN_STEREO_REPLICA_COPY=0. Mirrors of 2D outputs are unchanged.
 .
 On the VKMS rig (two HDMI outputs, anaglyph source, replica as frame packing
 of the same size, 1.5 times larger, plain 2D, letterboxed into 4:3) the
 replica makes no scene pass, its frame is pixel-identical to the one painted
 again at the same size, and within one level of it when scaled.
